I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.records.recover_json_object. Its documented rule says: “The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text, because a model places its final answer last and marks it: a reply that quotes its previous answer and then gives a corrected one is scored on the correction.” The two reply texts are as follows. The first reply text is "Here is a draft:\n```json\n{\"a\":1} {\"b\":2}\n```\nFinal answer: {\"c\":3}". The second reply text is "{\"c\":3}". For the first reply, the rule requires the object {"b":2}, because the code fence holds two objects and the last one inside the fence is {"b":2}. For the second reply, the rule requires the object {"c":3}, because there is no code fence holding an object, so the last top-level object is {"c":3}. The check recover_json_object returns {"c":3} for both replies: in the first reply it fails to parse the fenced content as a single JSON object, falls through to the top-level pool, and returns the last top-level object, which is {"c":3}; in the second reply it returns the only object. Thus the check gives the same answer on a pair the rule requires it to tell apart. I would be wrong if recover_json_object, given the first reply, returned {"b":2} instead of {"c":3}, or if the documented rule were read as not counting a fence that contains multiple objects as “any fence holds one.”